INT. CHILDREN'S PUPPET THEATER, BACKSTAGE - NIGHT

Dust. Velvet. Strings hanging like vines. Papier-mâché heads on hooks. One working bulb.

MARTA OSWIN, 30s, cleans a marionette with a rag. THEO MERCER, 20s, the stagehand, counts stage weights. A VOICE hums from the dark above the catwalk. A FIGURE in a cloak sits in the third row, outlined by the footlights, still as a coat.

MARTA
Theo, who's in the house?

THEO
No one. We locked up at nine.

MARTA
Then who's that?

THEO
That's the coat rack.

MARTA
The coat rack doesn't hum.

The humming stops. The cloak turns. A long white face tilts into the footlights. It has no mouth.

FIGURE
(a dry whisper)
Whose turn is it?

THEO
Marta.

MARTA
Don't move.

The marionette in her hands twitches. Its strings tighten by themselves. The head turns toward her.

MARTA (CONT'D)
(whispering)
Theo. Call Inez.

THEO
Inez is at home.

MARTA
Call her.

He thumbs a phone. It rings, tinny.

INEZ (V.O.)
(sleepy, over the phone)
Hello?

THEO
Inez, it's Theo. At the theater. Something's in the house.

INEZ (V.O.)
What kind of something?

THEO
A man. A thing. It's got a face like a plate.

INEZ (V.O.)
Theo. Get out of the theater.

THEO
I can't. It's between me and the door.

The figure rises from the seat. It steps up onto the stage, boot by boot. The bulb dims. Marta lets go of the puppet. It dangles, spinning slowly.

MARTA
It's the Plate Man.

THEO
The what?

MARTA
(backing away)
My grandmother told me. He takes the last one out of the house.

INEZ (V.O.)
Theo? Theo, are you there?

PLATE MAN
(a dry whisper)
Yours.

The Plate Man opens its cloak. Behind it, rows of small white faces. Theo screams.
